<spell-notebook "rating usage">
	<header level="1">
		Rating
		<header>A rating indicates user interest in content.</>
	</>
	<rating-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#events">Events</>
				<ui-item link="#forms">Forms</>
				<ui-item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
				<ui-item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, forms), keyboard, accessibility, framework snippets. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Write `<ui-rating>` and give it a name with `aria-label` or a `<label for>`: it's a form control, a group of real
			radios, one per icon. No script needed: Fomantic's `data-icon`, `data-rating` and `data-max-rating` metadata and
			its `icon`, `initialRating` and `maxRating` settings are plain attributes.

			- `icon` is an icon NAME (`star`, the default, `heart`, any `<ui-icon>` name), not a class.
			- `max-rating` is how many icons (Fomantic's default: 4).
			- `value` is the rating, `0` (none) ... `max-rating`. The attribute is the starting (and reset) value; the
			  `value` property is the live one.
			- `clearable` lets the user take the rating back by choosing the current one again; a one-icon rating is always
			  clearable (Fomantic's `clearable: auto`).
			- `readonly` and `disabled` are Fomantic's `interactive: false` and its `disable` / `enable` behaviours.
			- Fomantic's `set rating` / `get rating` / `clear rating` behaviours are the `value` property: set it, read it,
			  set it to `0`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			`ui-changed` fires when the user rates (Fomantic's `onRate`), with `event.detail.value`: `0` when they clear it.
			Setting `value` back inside the handler vetoes the change. Setting `value` from script fires nothing (Fomantic's
			`fireOnInit: false`).
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const rating = document.querySelector("ui-rating")
			rating.addEventListener("ui-changed", (event) => console.log(event.detail.value))
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Forms">
		<markdown>
			A rating is a form-associated element.

			- With a `name`, it submits its `value` with the form while it's above `0`.
			- `required` makes a rating necessary; the element is `:state(invalid)` once the user has interacted and it's
			  still empty.
			- A form reset puts back the starting `value` (the attribute).
			- `disabled` leaves it out of the form; `readonly` still submits.
			- A fractional `value` (`3.5`) only DRAWS a partial icon: no radio is chosen until the user rates.
		</>

		<section label="In a form" sticky collapsible dividing>
			<example description="A required rating, submitted as `food`.  Rate, then reset the form to put back the starting value.">
				<html>
					<ui-form>
						<form>
							<label for="rating-food">Food</label>
							<ui-rating id="rating-food" name="food" color="yellow" value="2" max-rating="5" required></ui-rating>
							<ui-button type="reset" icon="undo" appearance="basic">Reset</ui-button>
						</form>
					</ui-form>
				</html>
			</example>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			The rating is a radio group, so it's ONE tab stop and the arrows move the choice.

			| Key                      | Action                                    |
			| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------- |
			| Tab                      | Move into the rating (one stop), then out |
			| Arrow Right / Arrow Down | Choose one more (wraps to the first)      |
			| Arrow Left / Arrow Up    | Choose one less (wraps to the last)       |
			| Home / End               | Choose the lowest / highest rating        |
			| Space                    | Choose the focused icon                   |
			| Backspace / Delete       | Clear a `clearable` rating                |
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			The rating is a group of real radios: one per icon, each named "3 of 5" (translatable).

			- The group is a `radiogroup` named by the host's `aria-label` or a `<label for>`. A fractional rating adds a
			  description, "Rated 3.5 of 5".
			- `readonly` stays focusable and announced but can't change; `disabled` leaves the tab order.
			- Colour is never the only signal: filled and empty icons differ in shape, and the radios carry the count.
			- If the element can't render, its native fallback is the same group of real radios, numbered instead of icons,
			  so the rating still works and submits; colours, sizes, the hover preview, partial icons, `clearable` and
			  Home / End are dropped.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>`value` is a plain attribute; listen for `ui-changed` with your framework's custom-event syntax.</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-rating name="food" max-rating={5} value={stars} onui-changed={(event) => setStars(event.detail.value)} />
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-rating name="food" max-rating="5" :value="stars" @ui-changed="(event) => (stars = event.detail.value)" />
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<ui-rating
			  name="food"
			  max-rating="5"
			  value={stars()}
			  on-ui-changed={(event) => setStars(event.detail.value)}
			/>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
